isolated_halo
=============

An isolated disc galaxy in a live dark matter halo, from the bundled isodisk ICs. This is the only
PBHEF problem with self-gravity and a halo with a real velocity dispersion, so it is the one that
exercises two things the idealised problems cannot: donors that move appreciably between their own
timesteps, and a dark matter distribution far more extended than the gas.

35000 gas, 50000 halo, 20000 stellar disc, 10000 bulge and one sink, spanning about 130 kpc. Only
PartType1 carries black holes, which is the halo alone; the stellar disc and bulge are types 2 and 3
and are correctly left out.

Files
-----
  isolated_halo.params  GIZMO parameters; run_matrix.sh overrides OutputDir per run
  run_matrix.sh         builds and runs the six configurations
  runs/                 everything generated, one directory per configuration

The initial conditions are the bundled isodisk_ics, which are not kept in the repository. Any copy of
scripts/test_problems/ics/isodisk_ics.hdf5 will do.

Running
-------
From the repository root:

  scripts/test_problems/outputs/isolated_halo/run_matrix.sh 4

Naming configurations after the task count runs only those:

  scripts/test_problems/outputs/isolated_halo/run_matrix.sh 4 cool_base cool_pbhef1 cool_pbhef2

Six configurations, MFM throughout: no PBHEF, the receiver method and the donor method, each without
and with radiative cooling and star formation. About eight minutes a run on four tasks.

The ultraviolet background
--------------------------
The three cooling runs read the z=0 ultraviolet background from a TREECOOL file in the directory the
code is run from. GIZMO evaluates it even in a non-cosmological run, at z=0, so it is not optional
here. Check every cooling run's log for

  ..read ionization table [TREECOOL] with 204 non-zero UVB entries

A 0 there means the run had no background at all and has to be redone. The reader used to produce that
silently for any table still carrying its comment header, which is how the file is distributed; it now
skips comments and warns when the table comes out empty, but a truncated or wrong-format file can still
leave it short, so the count is worth reading.

Results
-------
Ratio of the energy the gas received to the analytic total the black holes radiated, over a run to
t = 0.25, and for the donor runs the lowest per-step fraction of its intended rate that found gas:

  config       injected/expected  worst-coupled
  pbhef1                0.045281  -
  pbhef2                0.328032  0.369110
  cool_pbhef1           0.279394  -
  cool_pbhef2           0.489539  0.698455

Two things to read here.

Without cooling the two methods disagree by a factor of seven, and both numbers are accounted for.
The donor is limited by how far it may widen its kernel, which is 100 * 2.8 * SofteningHalo, or 14 kpc
here: 68.97 per cent of the halo mass has no gas particle within 14 kpc, leaving 31.03 per cent
reachable, against a budget report of 0.3096 on the first steps. The receiver is limited by something
else entirely. Its coupled power is the dark matter density summed over the gas cells weighted by their
volumes, which is the dark matter mass lying inside the volume the gas occupies: 3.5 per cent of the
halo at the start. That is far below the 19.4 per cent inside a sphere of 25 kpc, because a cell's
volume is its mass over its density, so the gas volume is dominated by the diffuse outskirts while the
dark matter mass is centrally concentrated.

Both are faithful to their own definition. They disagree because "dark matter overlapping the gas" and
"dark matter within one kernel of some gas" are very different quantities once the gas is a thin disc
inside an extended halo.

With cooling and star formation both couple much more, and the gap narrows from a factor of seven to a
factor of under two. The cause is the sub-grid winds, which drive gas out into the halo where the dark
matter is. Measured at the end of each run:

  run           r_med   r_90  gas volume  DM within 14 kpc of gas
  pbhef1        10.68  23.73   1.819e+05                   0.3484
  cool_pbhef1   12.13  30.26   1.226e+06                   0.6369
  pbhef2        10.67  23.79   2.487e+05                   0.3693
  cool_pbhef2   12.63  34.40   2.294e+06                   0.7221

The gas volume grows by a factor of seven to nine, which is what the receiver integrates over, and the
share of the halo with gas within a donor's reach roughly doubles. So how much energy either method
couples in a galaxy is set by where the baryonic physics puts the gas, not by the evaporation model.
Any figure quoted for the coupled fraction has to name the feedback model it came with.

Why this is worse here than for annihilation feedback
----------------------------------------------------
List et al. found the two approaches agreed well on an isolated galaxy. Their rate goes as the square
of the dark matter density, and squaring concentrates the power towards the centre, where the gas is.
Weighting this halo both ways and asking how much lies within 14 kpc of any gas:

  linear in rho, as PBH evaporation is       0.3103
  quadratic in rho, as annihilation is       0.8870

So almost nine tenths of an annihilation signal comes from where the gas already is, and both of their
methods sample nearly the same thing. Barely a third of an evaporation signal does, because a one-body
rate weights the outer halo far more heavily, and that is exactly where there is no gas to heat. The
same difference that makes the two methods agree more closely than DMAF's on the idealised problems
makes them agree less closely on a realistic galaxy.
